WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.440 --> 00:00:06.800
Well, it happened: On May 19th, 2019, HBO's
celebrated fantasy series Game of Thrones

00:00:06.800 --> 00:00:08.180
officially came to an end.

00:00:08.460 --> 00:00:12.180
Meanwhile, the series of novels it's based
upon is far from complete.

00:00:12.360 --> 00:00:16.840
As you're no doubt aware, author George R.R.
Martin has yet to finish the final two books

00:00:16.840 --> 00:00:19.340
in his epic saga, A Song of Ice and Fire.

00:00:19.540 --> 00:00:24.280
Right now, The Winds of Winter and A Dream
of Spring are still very much works in progress.

00:00:24.290 --> 00:00:29.110
However, Martin made some rather interesting
comments regarding the end of the book series,

00:00:29.110 --> 00:00:32.340
and how it will ultimately compare to the
finale of the HBO show.

00:00:32.440 --> 00:00:35.920
But before we get too into that, please note:
Spoilers ahead!

00:00:36.800 --> 00:00:38.040
"I'll tell you everything."

00:00:38.860 --> 00:00:43.920
On May 20th, Martin shared a post on his personal
website that began on a rather sentimental

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:44.740
note, writing:

00:00:44.920 --> 00:00:49.300
"The last night, the last show...It is hard
to believe it is over, if truth be told."

00:00:49.480 --> 00:00:54.670
Elsewhere, he expressed gratitude to series
creators David Benioff and D.B. Weiss, offering

00:00:54.670 --> 00:00:57.880
thanks to everyone who helped make the HBO
show a reality:

00:00:58.160 --> 00:01:02.520
"It has been a wild ride, to say the least...There
were forty-two cast members at the season

00:01:02.520 --> 00:01:05.520
eight premiere in New York City, and that
wasn't even all of them.

00:01:05.800 --> 00:01:09.480
And the crew, though less visible than the
cast, were no less important."

00:01:09.610 --> 00:01:14.040
Then he offered a characteristically vague
progress report, assuring fans that he's still

00:01:14.040 --> 00:01:15.920
hard at work on finishing the books:

00:01:15.920 --> 00:01:19.700
"Let me say this much, last night was an ending,
but it was also a beginning.

00:01:19.860 --> 00:01:22.680
Nobody is retiring any time soon…And I'm
writing.

00:01:23.060 --> 00:01:26.220
Winter is coming, I told you, long ago…and
so it is."

00:01:26.520 --> 00:01:29.580
Meanwhile, he didn't commit to any firm dates,
writing:

00:01:29.580 --> 00:01:33.360
"The Winds of Winter is very late, I know,
I know, but it will be done.

00:01:33.640 --> 00:01:38.090
I won't say when, I've tried that before,
only to burn you all and jinx myself…but

00:01:38.090 --> 00:01:41.080
I will finish it, and then will come A Dream
of Spring."

00:01:41.520 --> 00:01:45.640
But here's the burning question: Will A Dream
of Spring have the same conclusion as the

00:01:45.640 --> 00:01:46.140
show?

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:49.480
Instead of a direct answer, Martin went the
cryptic route, writing:

00:01:49.700 --> 00:01:50.700
"Well…yes.

00:01:50.880 --> 00:01:51.720
And no.

00:01:51.860 --> 00:01:52.560
And yes.

00:01:52.580 --> 00:01:53.320
And no.

00:01:53.540 --> 00:01:54.120
And yes.

00:01:54.120 --> 00:01:54.920
And no.

00:01:55.080 --> 00:01:55.740
And yes."

00:02:02.280 --> 00:02:06.320
Well, at least Martin has a theory as to why
the books will inevitably differ from the

00:02:06.320 --> 00:02:07.320
HBO series:

00:02:07.440 --> 00:02:11.220
"I am working in a very different medium than
David and Dan, never forget."

00:02:11.420 --> 00:02:12.100
He's got a point.

00:02:12.500 --> 00:02:16.750
Where Benioff and Weiss wrapped up the final
season in six episodes, Martin's last two

00:02:16.750 --> 00:02:21.920
novels in the book series are expected to
span, quote, "3000 manuscript pages."

00:02:21.920 --> 00:02:23.130
As he sagely notes:

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:27.200
"There are characters who never made it onto
the screen at all, and others who died in

00:02:27.200 --> 00:02:28.940
the show but still live in the books."

00:02:29.160 --> 00:02:32.940
So if that's going to impact the end of the
book series, which ending should be considered

00:02:32.940 --> 00:02:33.700
the "real" one?

00:02:33.940 --> 00:02:39.100
Martin clearly thinks that's a silly question,
asking an evasive rhetorical question in response:

00:02:39.180 --> 00:02:41.260
"How many children did Scarlett O’Hara have?"

00:02:41.760 --> 00:02:46.340
We'll take the bait and answer that for you:
She had three children in the 1936 novel Gone

00:02:46.340 --> 00:02:51.360
with the Wind, one child in the 1939 film
based on the book, and none in real life…

00:02:51.460 --> 00:02:53.060
because she's not a real person.

00:02:53.200 --> 00:02:53.720
Got it?

00:02:57.880 --> 00:03:02.020
Martin's post echoes some of his past remarks
about the fundamental differences between

00:03:02.020 --> 00:03:03.780
the HBO series and the novels.

00:03:04.040 --> 00:03:05.720
Back in April, he told Rolling Stone:

00:03:05.960 --> 00:03:09.160
"Would I prefer [Benioff and Weiss] do it
exactly the way I did it?

00:03:09.160 --> 00:03:14.180
Sure…It's two variations of the same story,
or a similar story, and you get that whenever

00:03:14.180 --> 00:03:15.180
anything is adapted."

00:03:15.510 --> 00:03:19.420
When all is said and done, perhaps the biggest
takeaway from Martin's post comes from how

00:03:19.420 --> 00:03:20.920
he decided to sign off:

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:21.700
"How about this?

00:03:21.940 --> 00:03:22.520
I'll write it.

00:03:22.720 --> 00:03:23.220
You read it.

00:03:23.420 --> 00:03:27.180
Then everyone can make up their own mind and
argue about it on the internet."

00:03:27.180 --> 00:03:28.340
Sounds like a plan.

00:03:28.740 --> 00:03:32.880
In the meantime, who do you think will prove
a faster writer: George R.R. Martin or Brienne

00:03:32.880 --> 00:03:33.680
of Tarth?

